em must be true. So no, they can't both be true. And since Q being true leads to a contradiction with R being false, Q has to be false. Therefore, the only possible solution is indeed P=true, Q=false, R=false.\n</think>\n\nTo determine the truth values of \\( P \\), \\( Q \\), and \\( R \\) based on the given statements:\n\n1. **Exactly one of \\( P \\) or \\( Q \\) is true**  \n   This implies either:\n   - \\( P = \\text{true} \\) and \\( Q = \\text{false} \\)\n   - \\( P = \\text{false} \\) and \\( Q = \\text{true} \\)\n\n2. **If \\( Q \\) is true, then \\( R \\) is true**  \n   This translates to the conditional statement: \\( Q \\rightarrow R \\). If \\( Q \\) were true, \\( R \\) must also be true.\n\n3. **\\( R = \\text{false} \\)**  \n\n",
